// XFAIL: DF-218e a GENERIC function driven as a root cannot contain a nested
// suspending call — the callee is reported undefined at the user's own line
//
// `g.spawn(gworker(7))` where `gworker<T>` suspends only by `yield_now()`
// compiles and runs (sweep S1 row p08c). Give the same generic body a nested
// suspending CALL and the compiler reports `undefined function mk` at the call
// site the author wrote, followed by an undefined-variable cascade for the
// binding it feeds.
//
// MECHANISM (confirmed by design 237 unit 1's census, which was asked whether
// this was the ANF hoist's entry gap reached through a monomorphized body — it
// is NOT, and it exits design 237 on that finding):
//
//   * the error is emitted by the POST-TRANSFORM RE-CHECK (`-v` puts it after
//     "Applied coroutine transform; re-checking..."), not by the hoist and not
//     by codegen;
//   * a suspending nested callee is CONSUMED by the transform — it becomes a
//     frame plus a driver and the plain function is dropped from
//     `program.functions` — which is why the NON-GENERIC twin below compiles:
//     its own body is replaced by a frame too, so nothing names `mk` any more;
//   * a GENERIC declaration also leaves its un-transformed TEMPLATE in the
//     program, and that template's body still names `mk`. The re-check
//     typechecks it and finds nothing there.
//
// It is the template, not the instantiations: driving `gworker` at TWO type
// arguments produces exactly ONE `undefined function` error, not two.
//
// BOUNDARY, re-probed Aug 21 — WIDER than the original filing said. The
// AMBIENT-entry cell fails too (`__saw_drive(gworker(7))`, the second function
// below), so this is not the spawn path's handling of a generic root: it is
// every driven generic body with a nested suspending call. The two cells that
// still work are the ones with no consumed callee to lose (a generic body whose
// only suspension is `yield_now()`) and the non-generic twin.
//
// The fix owes the sweep obligation 4 asks for: generic roots that are METHODS,
// MT spawn, a generic root whose nested callee is itself generic, and a generic
// template that survives a transform which consumed a callee it names for any
// other reason.
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// v 1
// joined 0
// ambient v 1
// ambient 0
import std.task.{yield_now}

func mk(id: Int) -> Int {
    yield_now()
    id
}

func gworker<T>(seed: T) -> Int {
    let v1 = mk(1)
    print("v {v1}")
    let _ = move seed
    0
}

func gambient<T>(seed: T) -> Int {
    let v1 = mk(1)
    print("ambient v {v1}")
    let _ = move seed
    0
}

func main() {
    var g = TaskGroup()
    let h = g.spawn(gworker(7))
    print("joined {h.join()}")
    print("ambient {__saw_drive(gambient(9))}")
}
